### Lead P1
VERDICT: real. Fork code, but the cause is not the one the lead gives.
- The fallback's comment says "the Win32 one refuses an existing destination". That is false against this tree.
  - `retro_vfs_file_rename_impl` uses `MoveFileExW(..., MOVEFILE_REPLACE_EXISTING)` on Win32.
  - Legacy, Xbox and Wii U builds move the old file aside and put it back on failure (`vfs_implementation.c` 2049–2130, 2245–2265).
- So the first `filestream_rename` already replaces the file on every platform. The fallback at `task_save.c:427-432` only runs when a replacing rename failed for some *other* reason, such as a sharing violation from antivirus or a cloud-sync client.
- It then deletes the good destination and retries the same failing operation. That turns a safe failure into the case where `.tmp` is the only copy.
- Callers: `task_save_handler_finished:494` reports only "Failed to atomically replace save state". `content_auto_save_state:2035`, `task_save.c:2505` and `save.c:568` return false or `goto fail`.
- I searched for any load or start-up code that reads `<path>.tmp` and found none.
- The next save of the same path reopens `.tmp` for writing and truncates it, so the only copy is overwritten before the new write is known to be good.

### Lead P2
VERDICT: real. Same shape as P1, in vendored code.
- `file_stream.c:2270-2285` has the identical delete-then-retry and the same false comment.
- Callers: `save.c:574`, `task_save.c:2511`, `disk_index_file.c:434`, and the cloud-sync `webdav.c:948`, `s3.c:983`, `google_drive.c:1328`. Each takes only a bool back.
- No caller or start-up path recovers `.tmp`.
- This is also a fork-local change to `libretro-common/`, so it will be clobbered on re-sync.

### Lead T1
VERDICT: real (rendering defect), not out of bounds. The 54-byte offset and the no-padding assumption are upstream; the fork's size check and stride are consistent with them.
- The BMP comes from the AI-service server's reply (`response->image_data`), which is untrusted.
- The check bounds every read: `(uint64_t)image_width * image_height > (uint64_t)(new_image_size - 54) / 3`, with `new_image_size >= 54` checked first. So `memcpy(raw_image_data, raw_image_file_data + 54, w*h*3)` stays inside the buffer. The fork's `in_stride = -1 * (int)image_width * 3` matches the copied buffer.
- A crafted file therefore cannot read past the end on this path.
- What does break with a real BMP:
  - Padded rows (where `w*3 % 4 != 0`) shear the image.
  - `bfOffBits` (bytes 10–13) is ignored, so V4/V5 headers misplace the pixels.
  - `biBitCount` is unchecked, so a 32-bit BMP is read as 24-bit.
  - A negative height (top-down BMP) is rejected by the size check.

### Lead T2
VERDICT: upstream code, real but latent.
- With `step < 1`, `val = (unsigned)(min + (double)j * step)` gives repeated values, so the drop-down gets duplicate items and `m_hash[val]` is overwritten.
- The old float loop (`m_hash[i]`, `addItem(..., i)`) truncated the same way, so the fork did not introduce this.
- I did not establish whether any uint setting has a fractional step (see Open questions).

### Lead T3
VERDICT: real, LOW.
- I know of nothing in the BPS (beat) format that forbids a target size of zero. I did not open the spec text.
- `if (modify_target_size == 0) return PATCH_TARGET_ALLOC_FAILED;` rejects such a patch with a misleading "alloc failed" error.
- The NULL-write danger the comment gives is already covered by two things:
  - the following `|| !bps.target_data` branch allocates;
  - the per-action bound against `bps.target_length` (the comment at 290–300) stops any write when the length is 0.
- Line 263 is BPS-only. IPS and UPS are not affected by it.

### Lead V1
VERDICT: not a defect in the code. The comment is wrong.
- `wl_output_destroy` in client-side libwayland is a generated stub that calls `wl_proxy_destroy`. That only frees the proxy and sends no request. I am stating libwayland's behaviour from knowledge; I did not open its source.
- No listener fires, so nothing can re-enter. Unlinking before freeing is still correct.
- The added `if (oi)` / `if (oi->output)` guards are harmless.

### Lead V2
VERDICT: not a defect (debug output only).
- Both callers re-queue the buffer before calling `v4l2_frame_times`:
  - `source_v4l2_normal`: `VIDIOC_QBUF` at about line 1403, then `v4l2_frame_times(bufcp)` at 1407;
  - `source_v4l2_alternate_hack`: QBUF before 1517.
- So no buffer is lost on that return.
- When `strdup` fails, `ft_fcount++` has already run but `ft_favg` is not updated. Only the "On"-gated printout is skewed.

### Lead V3
VERDICT: not a defect.
- `math_matrix_4x4` is `{ float data[16]; }` (matrix_4x4.h:41–43). It has no padding, so `sizeof(quad->mvp->data) == sizeof(*quad->mvp)`.
- The copy (7666), the descriptor range (7677) and `vk->tracker.mvp = *quad->mvp` (7688) all agree.
- This only becomes a risk if the struct grows, and it has not.

